---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const IMG = '/img/snapshot-analysis';
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title="Do Snapshot Tests Help in Agent-Driven Development?"
  description="An empirical analysis of 311 pull requests in the openhands-cli repository, where 68% of PRs are authored by AI agents. We examine whether snapshot tests actually catch bugs, guard behavior, and improve the development process."
  date="May 18, 2026"
  image={`${IMG}/deep_10_bug_rate_with_context.png`}
  readTime="12 min read"
  tags={['AI Agents', 'Testing', 'Snapshot Tests', 'Empirical Analysis']}
>

  <p>
    We <a href="#method-mining">mined</a> all 311 merged PRs from the
    <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/openhands-cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openhands-cli</a>
    repository (Sep 2025 – May 2026), <a href="#method-classification">classified</a> each by
    type, authorship, and snapshot involvement, then analyzed
    <a href="#method-snapshot">commit-level history</a> to understand <em>how</em> snapshot tests
    are actually used. Snapshot tests were adopted in January 2026.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/0_summary_dashboard.png`} alt="Summary dashboard showing 311 PRs, 68% agent-authored, 46 touching snapshots" />
  </figure>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/3_snapshot_adoption_timeline.png`} alt="Snapshot adoption timeline" />
    <figcaption>Before January 2026, zero PRs touched snapshot files. After adoption, 19–33% of monthly PRs include snapshot changes.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Where Do Bugs Live?</h2>

  <p>
    Not all bugs are visual — and snapshot tests can only help with bugs that change rendered
    output. This distinction is critical: counting all bug-fixes against snapshot coverage
    inflates the apparent gap.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_16_bugfix_domain.png`} alt="Bug-fix domain breakdown" />
    <figcaption>The largest category is TUI widgets &amp; screens (38), where 12 involved snapshots (green). But 27 bugs (35%) are entirely non-visual: CLI core, config/state, API/auth. Snapshot tests are structurally irrelevant for these.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_17_severity_by_domain.png`} alt="Bug severity by domain" />
    <figcaption>Critical bugs skew non-visual (44%) — crashes, auth failures, dependency issues. UX regressions are predominantly visual (75%), the natural domain for snapshot tests.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>Framing matters.</strong> "85% of bug-fix PRs ship without touching snapshots" sounds
    damning, but 35% of those bugs are in code that snapshots <em>can't</em> cover — API auth,
    CLI argument parsing, dependency pinning. The honest denominator is the 51 visual bug-fixes,
    where 12 (24%) have snapshot involvement. That's still a coverage gap — but it's a different
    conversation than "85%."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What's Actually Covered?</h2>

  <p>
    Among the 51 visual (TUI) bug-fixes, what fraction hit paths that snapshot tests guard?
    We analyzed this at three levels of precision.
  </p>

  <h3>Level 1: Did the bug-fix PR touch snapshots?</h3>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_13_bugfix_coverage_gap.png`} alt="Bug-fix coverage gap" />
    <figcaption>Of all 78 bug-fixes, 66 (85%) ship without touching snapshots. 11 (14%) modified existing baselines. Only 1 (1%) added new coverage.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Level 2: Path-level analysis</h3>

  <p>
    Zooming into the 35 post-adoption TUI bug-fixes, we checked whether the bug-fix PR touched
    the same code <em>paths</em> covered by snapshots — not just the same files.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_15_covered_vs_uncovered.png`} alt="Path-level coverage breakdown" />
    <figcaption>34% hit directly covered paths (fix PR updates snapshots). 57% are in files with <em>some</em> snapshot coverage but the specific bug path was uncovered. 9% are fully uncovered.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Level 3: Accounting for indirect coverage</h3>

  <p>
    Snapshot tests don't just test rendering — they implicitly test the full stack behind what's
    rendered. A test that renders a conversation after switching implicitly exercises persistence.
    We <a href="#method-indirect">read every snapshot test file</a> (20 test classes + conftest) and asked:
    <em>would the snapshot output actually change if this bug existed?</em>
  </p>

  <p>Strict criteria for "indirectly covered":</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A snapshot test exercises a flow through this code path</li>
    <li>The bug would produce <strong>visibly different</strong> output</li>
    <li>The test's input data would trigger the bug</li>
  </ol>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_18_reclassified_coverage.png`} alt="Reclassified path-level coverage" />
    <figcaption>After accounting for indirect coverage: 46% of TUI bugs hit covered paths (up from 34%). 4 bugs are indirectly covered. 16 (46%) remain in adjacent-but-uncovered paths. <strong>Revised: 46% covered, 54% uncovered.</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Where Coverage Exists, It Works</h2>

  <p>
    The evidence for snapshot value is strongest in areas that have coverage.
    Three independent signals confirm this.
  </p>

  <h3>Forcing function: every feature must acknowledge visual impact</h3>

  <p>
    After adoption, <strong>46% of TUI-changing feature PRs</strong> include snapshot updates —
    up from 0%. Nearly every visual change passes through a "did you mean to change how this
    looks?" gate.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_4_forcing_function.png`} alt="Forcing function over time" />
    <figcaption>Feature PRs (green) climb to 100% snapshot involvement. Bug-fix PRs (red) follow at ~35–50% — lower because many bugs are non-visual or on uncovered paths.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Development guard: 58 "code first, fix snapshots later" commits</h3>

  <p>
    Of 27 multi-commit snapshot PRs, <strong>18 (67%)</strong> show code written first, then a
    later commit fixing broken snapshots — evidence that existing tests caught unintended
    visual changes during development.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_14_snapshot_guarding.png`} alt="Snapshot guarding behavior" />
    <figcaption>Feature PRs generated the most snapshot-fix commits (24, across 9 PRs). Refactors generated 11 — code restructuring was caught changing visual output.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>100% hit rate:</strong> 9 out of 9 multi-commit feature PRs touching snapshot-covered
    areas had to fix snapshot tests AFTER their initial code. Every single feature that touched
    covered areas had existing behavior guarded.
  </blockquote>

  <h3>Review speed: 2× faster first review</h3>

  <p>
    PRs with snapshot changes get first review in <strong>0.9 hours</strong> vs
    <strong>1.7 hours</strong> for non-snapshot PRs, despite being 8× larger by file count.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_6_review_speed.png`} alt="Review speed comparison" />
    <figcaption>Snapshot PRs get first review ~2× faster.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <strong>Caveat:</strong> This is correlational, not causal. Snapshot PRs may be faster to review
    because SVG diffs provide instant visual understanding — or because they're more routine,
    involve the same reviewers, or have different complexity profiles. The data doesn't distinguish
    these explanations.
  </p>

  <h2>The Bug Rate and Its Limits</h2>

  <p>
    Did snapshot tests reduce the UX regression rate? The answer depends on how you normalize.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/deep_10_bug_rate_with_context.png`} alt="Normalized UX regression rate" />
    <figcaption>UX bugs per cumulative TUI feature shipped. Pre-adoption peaked at 0.45. The settled post-adoption rate (Feb–Apr) drops to 0.02–0.10, which is 4–20× lower.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The raw per-month rate (% of agent PRs that are UX bugs) is misleading because monthly
    sample sizes range from 5 to 63. The bug-per-feature normalization controls for codebase growth
    and shows a clear drop once the adoption surge settles.
  </p>

  <p>
    But the overall TUI bug-to-feature ratio went from <strong>0.60 to 0.95</strong> —
    essentially flat. The severity mix shifted — critical bugs dropped while UX regressions rose —
    but this tracks codebase growth, not snapshot adoption.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${IMG}/5_bug_rate_timeline.png`} alt="Critical vs UX regression bug rate over time" />
    <figcaption>Critical bugs (red) drop to zero by March 2026. UX regressions (orange) spike
    in the adoption month then settle, before spiking again in May. Snapshot tests didn't reduce
    the total bug count because 54% of visual bugs are on uncovered paths.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Actionable Gaps</h2>

  <p>
    The data points to specific, fixable coverage gaps — not a blanket "write more tests" recommendation.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Settings persistence (7 bugs, 1 test would cover)</h3>

  <p>
    7 of 16 adjacent-but-uncovered bugs are in <code>settings_screen.py</code> and related modules.
    The repo tests the <em>first-time setup flow</em> (no saved config) and the
    <em>critic settings tab</em>, but no test saves settings, closes the modal, and reopens to
    verify persistence. A single "save → reload → snapshot" test would indirectly cover model
    name persistence, condensation defaults, dropdown ordering, and clear behavior.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Missing event types in test trajectories (4 bugs)</h3>

  <p>
    The e2e snapshot tests use recorded LLM trajectories for deterministic replay. But the current
    trajectories don't include <code>ConversationErrorEvent</code>, messages with markup
    characters, or critic-enabled <code>MessageEvent</code>. Adding 2–3 trajectories with these
    event types would extend coverage to code paths already exercised by the test framework but
    never triggered by the test data.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Notification rendering (2 bugs)</h3>

  <p>
    No snapshot test captures notification display (toast-style overlays). Settings change
    notifications and conversation notifications both had bugs that went uncaught.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Bug-fix coverage discipline</h3>

  <p>
    Only 1 of 78 bug-fix PRs added new snapshot coverage retroactively.
    When a visual bug is fixed, adding a snapshot that captures the corrected rendering
    would prevent the exact recurrence — the highest-value coverage extension possible.
  </p>

  <h2>Verdict</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Snapshot tests work where they exist — but coverage is shallow.</strong>
  </p>

  <p>The evidence for snapshot value is concrete and multi-signal:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>100% of feature PRs</strong> in covered areas had to fix broken snapshots during development (9/9 multi-commit feature PRs). Existing behavior was guarded.</li>
    <li><strong>58 snapshot-fix commits</strong> across 20 PRs show developers being forced to acknowledge visual changes at the commit level.</li>
    <li>The <strong>normalized UX regression rate dropped 4–20×</strong> in the settled post-adoption period (Feb–Apr 2026) compared to the pre-adoption peak.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>But coverage is shallow:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>54% of TUI bugs</strong> (after accounting for indirect coverage) are on uncovered code paths — mostly settings persistence and untested event types.</li>
    <li><strong>Only 1 of 78 bug-fix PRs</strong> added new snapshot coverage retroactively.</li>
    <li><strong>35% of all bugs are non-visual</strong> — snapshots are structurally irrelevant for API crashes, auth failures, and dependency issues.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The biggest ROI: a handful of targeted tests (settings save→reload, new event type
    trajectories, notification capture) would close the majority of the coverage gap with
    minimal effort.
  </p>

  <h2>Methodology</h2>

  <p>
    Everything in this analysis is reproducible from two scripts and a GitHub token.
    The full pipeline, data, and graphs are at
    <a href="https://github.com/malhotra5/snapshot-tests-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapshot-tests-analysis</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="method-mining">Step 1: Mining (GitHub API → JSON)</h3>

  <p>
    <code>scripts/mine_github.py</code> pulls every merged PR from
    <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/openhands-cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openhands-cli</a>
    via the GitHub REST API and <code>gh</code> CLI. For each PR it collects:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>PR metadata</strong> — title, body, author, labels, branch names, created/merged timestamps, additions/deletions, changed file count</li>
    <li><strong>File-level diffs</strong> — every file changed with its status (added/modified/removed) and line counts</li>
    <li><strong>Commit history</strong> — full commit messages per PR, which enabled the "code first, fix snapshots later" analysis</li>
    <li><strong>Review data</strong> — reviewer logins, review decision, time-to-first-review</li>
    <li><strong>Linked issues</strong> — cross-referenced issue titles, bodies, and authors</li>
    <li><strong>Release mapping</strong> — which release tag (if any) includes each PR</li>
    <li><strong>Authorship detection</strong> — heuristic markers for agent-authored PRs (co-authored-by tags, conversation links, bot authors)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Output: a single <code>mined_prs.json</code> file with 42 fields per PR across all 311 merged PRs.
    No LLM is involved in this step — it's pure API data.
  </p>

  <h3 id="method-classification">Step 2: Classification (LLM + heuristics → CSV)</h3>

  <p>
    <code>scripts/categorize_prs.py</code> classifies each PR along four dimensions.
    It runs two classifiers in parallel and keeps both outputs:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Heuristic classifier</strong> (keyword-based, no LLM):</p>
  <ul>
    <li>PR type — title keyword matching ("fix" → bug-fix, "feat" → feature, "bump" → version-bump, etc.)</li>
    <li>Bug severity — title keyword matching ("crash" → critical, "display" → ux-regression, "typo" → polish)</li>
    <li>Snapshot involvement — file path matching (<code>*.svg</code> in <code>snapshot</code> directories)</li>
    <li>Agent authorship — co-author tags and bot usernames</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>LLM classifier</strong> (gpt-4o-mini via <a href="https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenHands SDK</a>):</p>
  <ul>
    <li>PR type — same categories but with full context (title, description, file list, linked issues)</li>
    <li>Bug severity — critical / ux-regression / polish, with definitions in the system prompt</li>
    <li>Whether the PR touches TUI code — the LLM sees the actual file paths and description, catching cases keywords miss</li>
    <li>Rationale — a 1-2 sentence explanation stored alongside each classification for auditability</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The report uses the LLM classifications (<code>llm_pr_type</code>, <code>llm_bug_severity</code>,
    <code>llm_touches_tui</code>) as the primary labels, falling back to heuristics when the LLM
    call fails. The heuristic labels are retained in the CSV for comparison.
  </p>

  <h3 id="method-snapshot">Step 3: Snapshot-specific analysis (heuristic)</h3>

  <p>
    Several analyses required no LLM — just reading file paths, commit messages, and timestamps:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Commit-level pattern detection</strong> — for each multi-commit PR, check if snapshot-related keywords
    ("snapshot", "update snap", "regenerate", "baseline") appear only in later commits, indicating
    the "code first, fix snapshots later" pattern</li>
    <li><strong>Bug-fix coverage gap</strong> — for each bug-fix PR, classify as "no snapshots touched" / "modified existing" /
    "added new" based on the Git status of snapshot SVG files</li>
    <li><strong>Suite reuse</strong> — extract test suite names from snapshot file paths and count unique PRs per suite</li>
    <li><strong>Review speed</strong> — compare <code>hours_to_first_review</code> between snapshot and non-snapshot PRs</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="method-indirect">Step 4: Indirect coverage analysis (manual + scripted)</h3>

  <p>
    This was the most labor-intensive step. We cloned openhands-cli and read all 20 snapshot test
    files (~2,500 lines of test code), including <code>conftest.py</code> fixtures and trajectory
    replay helpers. From this, <code>scripts/analyze_indirect_coverage.py</code> builds:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>coverage map</strong> — which test suite exercises which functional behaviors
    (e.g., <code>TestConfirmationMode</code> exercises scrolling, confirmation panel, agent execution)</li>
    <li>A <strong>per-bug classification</strong> — for each of the 20 "file covered, path uncovered" bugs,
    whether a snapshot test would <em>actually produce different output</em> if the bug existed.
    Each classification includes a written rationale.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 5: Visualization</h3>

  <p>
    <code>scripts/generate_deep_analysis_graphs.py</code> produces all 18 matplotlib graphs.
    <code>scripts/generate_report.py</code> assembles the self-contained HTML report with
    base64-embedded images. Both are runnable with <code>uv run</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>LLM classification accuracy</strong> — gpt-4o-mini misclassifies some edge cases (e.g., a PR
    titled "fix: add feature X" gets classified as bug-fix). We spot-checked ~30 PRs and found
    ~90% agreement with human judgment. The heuristic labels provide a second opinion.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent authorship detection</strong> — relies on co-author tags and known bot usernames.
    PRs where a human manually committed agent-generated code would be counted as human-authored.</li>
    <li><strong>Indirect coverage is subjective</strong> — our B1 (indirectly covered) classifications
    are judgment calls about whether a snapshot would visibly change. Different reviewers might
    classify 1-2 borderline cases differently, but the overall split (4 B1 vs 16 B2) is robust.</li>
    <li><strong>Single repository</strong> — all findings are specific to openhands-cli. Generalizing to
    other agent-driven projects requires similar analysis in those codebases.</li>
  </ul>

</BlogPostLayout>
